!!schema:"https://tson.io/2026/37/io/ltr8/http/deployment.tn"
@doc:"""
  The validator demo's own deployment descriptor.

  It is NOT published and NOT resolvable: no schema imports it, no data document names it, and the catalog
  never serves it. What a caller may see is the projection at /.well-known/tson-deployment, derived from the
  policy this configures by TsonDeployment.profile. That is deployment.tn's rule 2, and this file is where it
  would be broken if it were going to be.

  The policy is stated whole, as policy.tn's `policy`, and runs exactly as written. Two parts are the
  library's defaults written out -- the identifier policy and the nesting bound -- and that is the point of
  stating them: a library upgrade that moved a default would not move this deployment.

  `token_policy` is set to something visibly strict so the demo demonstrates a deployment decision rather
  than describing one: submit a document with a mixed-script value and this deployment refuses it. Lower it
  to UNRESTRICTED and the same document conforms. That is the whole argument for the artifact, in a form you
  can run.

  Note where the annotations sit. [TSON-DATA] §2.5 admits no directive, annotation or type annotation before a
  FIELD NAME -- metadata about a field attaches to the field's VALUE. Writing `@doc:"..." token_policy: {...}`
  is a parse error, and it is the natural thing to reach for after writing a few schemas, where the entry
  position takes one.
  """
!deployment {
  name: "validator-demo"

  listener: { port: 8080 }

  policy: {
    identifier_policy: @doc:"""
        The library's default, written out: UTS #39's Highly Restrictive over the whole of each declared
        name, with the look-alike rule on.
        """
      { level: HIGHLY_RESTRICTIVE  per_segment: false  skeleton_distinctness: true  permitting: [] }

    token_policy: @doc:"""
        Stricter than the default, which is unrestricted. A service that renders or matches what it reads
        faces on values the spoofing surface [TSON-DATA] §9.4 raises for names, and §8.2 says such a
        deployment applies a level knowingly. SINGLE_SCRIPT refuses a token that mixes scripts: a wholly
        Cyrillic display name is fine, and a Latin word with one Cyrillic letter in it is not.
        """
      { level: SINGLE_SCRIPT  permitting: [] }

    limits: @doc:"""
        [TSON-DATA] §9.1's resource limits at the library's own default. A validator is exactly the service
        that should bound what it reads -- both the schema and the document come from the request -- so the
        posture is worth writing down even where it changes nothing.

        64 and not less, deliberately. This demo is a conformance tool: it runs the same scenarios the
        TypeScript implementation runs in a browser so the two verdicts can be compared, and a bound tighter
        than §9.1's default would make them disagree about a deep document for a reason that is this
        deployment's and not the format's.
        """
      { max_depth: 64 }
  }

  schema_hosts: @doc:"Empty, and a real posture: this service resolves only the schema sent with a request." []
}
